 "style": {
  "name": "Yellow terracotta twin-gable merchant shophouse",
  "rationale": "Second pass against the concept, looking from the street. (1) The roof read as one heavy mass and the gables as stray yellow chunks. Now each bay is a clear stepped mud-brick gable. The roof runs the full depth z0-z11, and the gable infill is set back to z1 (and z10 at the back), so the roof verge overhangs inside the lot and casts a soffit shadow over a 2x2 attic window. That recalls the concept's dormers without projecting past z=0. (2) The right side was the largest plain surface, so it now has a full storey grammar: dark oak corner posts, a floor beam at y5 (axis=z) wrapping from the front, two recessed ground windows with plank lintels, a recessed side door to the living quarters (the 'home above' entrance), two shuttered and potted upper windows, a pier between them that mirrors the front, and the stone cornice carried round. Shutters are open trapdoors set against the inner edge (facing=east), so they read as inset panels. (3) The back repeats the front's upper windows and shutters, has two recessed back doors beside the centre pier, two ground windows, the beam and the cornice. (4) Shopfronts: a 2-deep covered porch under awnings striped across both rows (orange/white and blue/white), dark oak risers, glass, a lintel and transom at z2, doors beside the terracotta pier, and hanging signs. A stone-brick slab at y0 gives the whole block a plinth line. Palette hierarchy is kept: yellow terracotta dominant; dark oak for posts, beams, shutters and frames; stone only for cornice and plinth; wool as trade colours. Both bays are consistent and mirrored. Nothing leaves 0..11, and the ridge slab tops out at y13."
 },
